    13	  # A cap_bound paper must not be rejected.  scripts/harden.py now DETECTS this
    14	  # rather than waiting for escalate() to declare it: if the ladder stopped with the
    15	  # answer near the output cap, or never moved more than one dial, the binding
    16	  # constraint was our answer FORMAT, not the paper.  2601.05272 climbed n=64..84,
    17	  # stopped at 249 atoms against a 256 cap, reported "cannot be made harder", and was
    18	  # rejected.  Instruction alone did not prevent that; this guard does.
    19	  # A G9(b) rejection blames the PAPER for a result produced by the builder's own
    20	  # hint.  Measured over the 17 modules on disk that carry a STRUCTURAL_HINT: 4 of 9
    21	  # rejected papers hand the solver a procedure, against 1 of 8 accepted.  This gate
    22	  # only ever runs on --reject, so that one accepted paper is never touched by it.
    23	  #
    24	  # 1904.00563 is the pattern: "Among target-one vertices, keep edges whose endpoints
    25	  # have equal eligible degree; the result is three even cycles, so take alternating
    26	  # edges on each."  That is the whole algorithm, including the derived count and the
    27	  # final step.  The hinted oracle solving it says nothing about the paper.
