/tmp/burning-paper.oKOg4Q/src/arxiv.tex:400:If $G$ has diameter at most $2$, then $b(G)=\bg(G)$.
/tmp/burning-paper.oKOg4Q/src/arxiv.tex:403:If the diameter of $G$ is $1$, then $G$ is a complete graph on at least two vertices for which $b(G)=\bg(G)=2$.
/tmp/burning-paper.oKOg4Q/src/arxiv.tex:405:Now suppose that the diameter is $2$, hence every burning process, whether it is the usual burning or the burning game, will finish in at least $2$ and in at most $3$ rounds. By Proposition~\ref{prop:characterize-1-2}, $\bg(G)=2$ if and only if $G \neq K_1$ and $\Delta(G) \geq |V(G)| - 2$. We already know that $G \neq K_1$ because of the diameter being $2$, hence $G$ must be a complete graph on at least two vertices without a matching. In such a graph we can burn a vertex that spreads to all but one vertex in the second round. Hence in the second round we only have one vertex to choose for the second source, i.e.\ $b(G)=2$.
/tmp/burning-paper.oKOg4Q/src/arxiv.tex:419:\begin{proposition}
