     1	# Burning game — verified generator for arXiv:2409.11328
     2	
     3	> Status: every local gate passes. The required oracle evidence is **blocked**, not
     4	> passed: all bare, structural-hint, and placebo calls returned HTTP 403 because the
     5	> configured OpenRouter key had reached its total limit. Error transcripts are kept,
     6	> but zero API errors are counted as model failures.
     7	
     8	## Profile
     9	
    10	| field | value |
    11	|---|---|
    12	| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
    13	| Native domain | combinatorics |
    14	| Object regime | finite discrete |
    15	| Computational core | graph |
    16	| Certificate form | integer tuple, compactly encoded as a fixed-width word |
    17	| Intended intuition | symmetry: one hidden relabelling is shared across many games |
    18	| Domain essentiality | native |
    19	| Reduction | none |
    20	
    21	## Problem and trust model
    22	
    23	The source is Chiarelli, Iršič, Jakovac, Kinnersley, and Mikalački,
    24	[*Burning game*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.11328). Section 1 defines the game on a
    25	finite simple graph: fire spreads at the start of a round, then Burner and Staller
    26	alternately ignite one unburned vertex. For every target graph, the solver must give
    27	a Burner first move that guarantees completion by round 3 against every Staller
    28	reply.
    29	
    30	The generator uses inverse construction plus a certificate-preserving
    31	transformation. In each reference graph it constructs a unique Proposition 10
    32	winner from named vertex roles; it never searches the finished graph for that
    33	winner. One common random vertex permutation is then applied to every target graph,
    34	and the known moves are carried through it. `verify` never reads `inst["answer"]`:
    35	it evaluates Proposition 10's exact maximum-degree and neighborhood condition for
    36	every submitted move. The self-test also compares that condition with the literal
    37	three-round game tree on all demo candidates.
    38	
    39	## Why Track B
    40	
    41	This paper proves no distributional hardness result for computing the new game
    42	burning number, so Track A would be unsupported. Proposition 10 instead gives the
    43	honest standard algorithm: scan all vertices and test its first- and
    44	second-neighborhood condition. At the shipping preset (`g=48`, `n=31`), the scan is
    45	`O(g n^3)` in the adjacency representation and averaged **74,524 exact adjacency
    46	operations and about 0.006 seconds** over eight seeds. It solved 8/8, as expected.
    47	
    48	The compact route reads each vertex's displayed degree in the first four reference
    49	and target graphs. Those cross-layer profiles are unique and preserved by the one
    50	shared relabelling. Matching them transports all 48 audited reference moves in at
    51	most `2*4*31 + 48 = 296` degree reads/lookups. Without recognizing the shared
    52	symmetry, the solver must repeat the Proposition 10 scan for every target.
    53	
    54	A construction-aware graph attack also succeeds, as Track B requires us to admit:
    55	one-dimensional Weisfeiler–Leman color refinement uniquely aligns every individual
    56	reference/target pair. On the same eight shipping seeds, the simple exact
    57	implementation solved 8/8 with a mean of **86,645 reads/updates and about 0.006 seconds**.
    58	That is still a mechanical route far beyond unaided execution, and it is reported
    59	beside—not hidden inside—the failing attack panel.
    60	
    61	The easy regimes identified by the paper were avoided: Proposition 9 characterizes
    62	game burning numbers 1 and 2 by degrees; Proposition 11 collapses diameter-at-most-2
    63	graphs to ordinary burning; Theorems 23 and 24 give direct path/cycle strategies;
    64	and Theorem 29 writes down the hypercube sources. The present gadget instead has
    65	exactly one three-round winning first move. Its winner is degree-tied with at least
    66	two decoys, while the maximum-degree and largest-two-neighborhood vertices are
    67	losing.
    68	
    69	## Worked demo
    70	
    71	This is `render(make_instance(seed=3, **DIFFICULTY["demo"]))` in full. A person can
    72	solve it on paper: form the four-degree profile of each reference label and match it
    73	to the corresponding target profile, then transport the four audited moves.
